// Copy this file to Local.xcconfig (gitignored, never committed) and fill in
// your own bundle identifier. script/bootstrap does this for you
// automatically if Local.xcconfig doesn't exist yet.
//
// Signing team does NOT belong here. XcodeGen reads whatever DEVELOPMENT_TEAM
// it can resolve at `generate` time — including one set via configFiles like
// this — and writes it into TargetAttributes in the shared, tracked
// project.pbxproj (confirmed: `xcodegen generate` with DEVELOPMENT_TEAM set
// in this file added `DevelopmentTeam = <id>;` to both targets in
// project.pbxproj). Since script/bootstrap runs `xcodegen generate` on every
// build, that would leak a real team ID into version control on every
// regeneration. Team ID instead lives in the repo-root `.env` (gitignored,
// see .env.example) and is passed to xcodebuild only as a trailing
// command-line override by script/run — never through project.yml/xcconfig.

PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER = com.example.Monkey

// The app group ID is a literal string in three places: Monkey.entitlements,
// MonkeyCLI.entitlements, and Sources/MonkeyCore/AppGroupStorage.swift (the
// Swift constant is runtime code and can't see build settings, so the
// entitlements just match it). If you change PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER above,
// update all three to match, then register the App Group once in Xcode's
// Signing & Capabilities tab for BOTH the iOS and macOS rows. If the app
// then still gets "Operation not permitted" writing to its container, delete
// the cached wildcard "Mac Team Provisioning Profile: *" from
// ~/Library/Developer/Xcode/UserData/Provisioning Profiles/ and rebuild —
// see PLAN.md §3a for why.
